            %p
              to be an isomorphism, where the vertical arrows are invertible symmetry constraints and the maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L u
              are isomorphisms. Now apply the equivalence between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext 4.
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{4.}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext 1.
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{1.}
              from the reflection theorem.
          %p
          .num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is cartesian closed, and the reflector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \colon D \to C
              preserves products, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is cartesian closed. Conversely, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is cartesian closed and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+functor"} dense functor
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              We have already seen under the hypotheses that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c, c'
              are in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^{c'}
              as calculated in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              inherits products from
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \colon C \to D
              is monadic and monadic functors reflect limits. The adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ×
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(c'' \times c', c) \cong C(c'', c^{c'})
              holds because it holds when interpreted in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is fully embedded in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %p
              For the converse, you can see Theorem 3.10 of
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Street80"} Street 1980
          %h2#reference Reference
          %ul
            %li#Day72
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"}> Brian Day
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A reflection theorem for closed categories
                %strong 2
                1 (1972), 1-11. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(72)90021-7"}> doi:10.1016/0022-4049(72)90021-7
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Street80
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cosmoi of internal categories
                %strong 258
                2 (1980) 271-318 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1998059"}> doi:10.2307/1998059
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            A version for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skew+monoidal+categories"} skew monoidal categories
            is given in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"} Steve Lack
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
              = succeed "," do
                %em Skew-monoidal reflection and lifting theorems
